  "content": {
    "id": "p66-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "$P(x)=x^3-6x+4$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p66-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find a factor of $P(x)$ using the factor theorem by testing the factors of $4$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x-2$, since $P(2)=0$.",
          "worked": "Possible integer roots are $\\pm1,\\pm2,\\pm4$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1-6+4=-1\\\\P(-1)&=-1+6+4=9\\\\P(2)&=8-12+4=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $x-2$ is a factor.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p66-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Express $P(x)$ as the product of a linear and quadratic factor.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=(x-2)(x^2+2x-2)$.",
          "worked": "Divide $x^3+0x^2-6x+4$ by $x-2$.\nThe first quotient term is $x^2$. Subtracting $x^2(x-2)$ leaves $2x^2-6x+4$.\nThe next quotient term is $2x$. Subtracting $2x(x-2)$ leaves $-2x+4$.\nThe final quotient term is $-2$. Subtracting $-2(x-2)$ leaves remainder $0$.\nTherefore\n$$P(x)=(x-2)(x^2+2x-2).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p66-q15-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence solve $P(x)=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=2,-1\\pm\\sqrt3$.",
          "worked": "$$(x-2)(x^2+2x-2)=0.$$ \nBy the null factor law, $x=2$ or $x^2+2x-2=0$.\nFor the quadratic,\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(-2)}}{2}\\\\&=\\frac{-2\\pm\\sqrt{12}}{2}\\\\&=-1\\pm\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $x=2,-1\\pm\\sqrt3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
